1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,640
A new phone has more switches inside

2
00:00:02,660 --> 00:00:04,840
it than there are people on Earth.

3
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:07,120
You've never seen any of them.

4
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:08,800
Each one is thousands of

5
00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:10,520
times thinner than a hair.

6
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:13,880
And every single one is made from sand.

7
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:16,560
Today, Bob finds out how.

8
00:00:17,390 --> 00:00:19,370
This is stop seven.

9
00:00:19,390 --> 00:00:21,450
Bob has finally left the desert,

10
00:00:21,470 --> 00:00:23,070
and the fog is gone.

11
00:00:23,470 --> 00:00:24,790
Welcome to the Foundry.

12
00:00:24,830 --> 00:00:27,070
This is where computers stop being an idea,

13
00:00:27,090 --> 00:00:29,310
and start being something you can hold.

14
00:00:29,330 --> 00:00:31,450
Back in the desert, everything

15
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:32,970
came down to switches.

16
00:00:33,470 --> 00:00:35,750
On is one, off is zero.

17
00:00:36,130 --> 00:00:37,970
But every switch Bob used,

18
00:00:37,990 --> 00:00:39,230
he flipped himself.

19
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:40,870
When he sent his face across

20
00:00:40,890 --> 00:00:42,370
the canyon, he was flipping

21
00:00:42,390 --> 00:00:44,430
lamps until the sun went down.

22
00:00:44,710 --> 00:00:46,730
A computer has to flip billions of

23
00:00:46,750 --> 00:00:49,770
switches every second, with no hands.

24
00:00:49,990 --> 00:00:51,970
So Bob needs a switch that electricity

25
00:00:51,990 --> 00:00:54,070
can flip, instead of a finger.

26
00:00:54,090 --> 00:00:57,530
Think of electricity like water in a pipe.

27
00:00:57,750 --> 00:00:59,190
When nothing is in the way, it

28
00:00:59,210 --> 00:01:00,930
flows all the way around, and

29
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:02,790
the lamp at the end lights up.

30
00:01:03,010 --> 00:01:04,490
A switch is just a gap

31
00:01:04,510 --> 00:01:05,970
you can open and close.

32
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:08,590
Close the gap, and electricity flows.

33
00:01:08,810 --> 00:01:10,070
That's a one.

34
00:01:10,090 --> 00:01:12,310
Open the gap, and nothing gets through.

35
00:01:12,510 --> 00:01:14,008
That's a zero.

36
00:01:14,208 --> 00:01:16,168
Some materials let electricity through

37
00:01:16,188 --> 00:01:18,328
really easily, like copper.

38
00:01:18,548 --> 00:01:20,268
That's why wires are made of it.

39
00:01:20,368 --> 00:01:22,048
Others, like glass, block

40
00:01:22,068 --> 00:01:23,528
it almost completely.

41
00:01:23,968 --> 00:01:25,448
And then there's silicon.

42
00:01:25,968 --> 00:01:27,748
Silicon comes from sand, and

43
00:01:27,768 --> 00:01:29,088
it's somewhere in the middle.

44
00:01:29,308 --> 00:01:30,428
It's picky.

45
00:01:30,548 --> 00:01:32,648
With the right tricks, you get to decide

46
00:01:32,668 --> 00:01:34,568
when it lets electricity through.

47
00:01:35,038 --> 00:01:36,758
So, put a tiny piece of

48
00:01:36,778 --> 00:01:38,598
silicon between two wires.

49
00:01:38,838 --> 00:01:41,018
On its own, it blocks the electricity.

50
00:01:41,338 --> 00:01:42,758
The gap is open.

51
00:01:43,018 --> 00:01:45,198
Now add a third wire on top.

52
00:01:45,478 --> 00:01:47,298
Send a little electricity into that

53
00:01:47,318 --> 00:01:48,918
one, and the silicon underneath

54
00:01:48,938 --> 00:01:50,958
starts letting electricity through.

55
00:01:51,118 --> 00:01:52,418
The gap closes.

56
00:01:52,818 --> 00:01:55,118
Turn it off, and the gap opens again.

57
00:01:55,358 --> 00:01:56,738
That's a transistor.

58
00:01:57,018 --> 00:01:58,658
A switch with no lever.

59
00:01:59,378 --> 00:02:00,358
Electricity flips it.

60
00:02:00,888 --> 00:02:02,628
This is the part that matters.

61
00:02:02,808 --> 00:02:04,448
The electricity coming out of one

62
00:02:04,468 --> 00:02:06,688
transistor can flip the next one.

63
00:02:06,708 --> 00:02:08,528
And that one can flip the next.

64
00:02:09,208 --> 00:02:10,568
Switches flipping switches,

65
00:02:10,708 --> 00:02:12,628
and nobody touches anything.

66
00:02:12,928 --> 00:02:14,268
Pretty much everything a computer

67
00:02:14,288 --> 00:02:16,848
does is built on that one idea.

68
00:02:17,518 --> 00:02:18,938
Of course, nobody builds

69
00:02:18,958 --> 00:02:20,518
these one at a time.

70
00:02:20,758 --> 00:02:22,718
First, the sand gets melted down

71
00:02:22,738 --> 00:02:25,678
and cleaned, over and over, until

72
00:02:25,698 --> 00:02:28,398
the silicon is almost perfectly pure.

73
00:02:28,778 --> 00:02:31,378
It's grown into one big shiny crystal,

74
00:02:31,398 --> 00:02:34,378
and sliced into discs thinner than a coin.

75
00:02:34,738 --> 00:02:36,838
Then billions of transistors get

76
00:02:36,858 --> 00:02:39,438
printed onto each disc, using light.

77
00:02:39,778 --> 00:02:42,238
Every little square on there is a chip.

78
00:02:42,298 --> 00:02:43,998
Remember how long it took

79
00:02:44,018 --> 00:02:45,315
Bob to send his face?

80
00:02:45,335 --> 00:02:46,498
Seven hundred and sixty-eight

81
00:02:46,518 --> 00:02:49,178
numbers, eight lamps each.

82
00:02:49,418 --> 00:02:51,238
A chip could flip every one of those

83
00:02:51,258 --> 00:02:53,318
switches before you finish blinking.

84
00:02:54,358 --> 00:02:56,218
Bob plants flag number seven.

85
00:02:56,238 --> 00:02:58,318
His first flag in the Foundry.

86
00:02:58,518 --> 00:03:00,118
Next time, he wires a few of

87
00:03:00,138 --> 00:03:01,458
these switches together, and

88
00:03:01,478 --> 00:03:02,878
they start making decisions.
